.TH TIP 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
tip \- connect to a remote system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tip
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tip
establishes a full-duplex connection to another machine, giving the
appearance of being logged in directly on the remote CPU.
Typed characters are normally transmitted directly to the remote machine.
.PP
This maux version connects to the installed serial device /dev/ttya0.
There are no phone-number, speed, remote-database or device operands.
The serial hardware and the other end must already be configured.
The local console is put in raw, no-echo mode for the session.
.PP
Escapes are recognized after Return, or at the start of the session:
.TP
.B ~.
Disconnect and restore the local terminal mode.
.TP
.B ~?
List escapes.
.TP
.B ~~
Send one literal tilde.
.PP
Other tilde sequences are sent literally.
Control-C and Control-Z are forwarded as bytes to the remote end,
not used to terminate or suspend the local tip session.
Incoming characters have their high bit stripped and are displayed using
the console's VT-style CR/LF handling.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/TIP  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
stty(1), reset(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 after a normal disconnect, 1 if the serial device cannot be opened or the receiver process cannot be started.
.SH BUGS
This is an interactive seven-bit terminal connection, not a binary file-transfer protocol. There is no modem dialling, baud-selection option, remote host database or cu alias.
.PP
Flow control is in band.
When the local receive buffer fills,
.I tip
sends DC3 (XOFF) to the remote end, and DC1 (XON) when it drains.
A remote program that clears IXON on its terminal, such as vim 9.0,
receives these as keystrokes: DC1 is CTRL-Q, which starts blockwise Visual mode in vim.
The serial hardware cannot throttle with RTS and still transmit,
so there is no hardware flow control.
Use vim 9.1 or later, or a remote program that leaves IXON set.
